  {
    "episode_id": 80548,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/0/80548.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 2,
    "turn_text": "Thanks. So we're talking at the end of July. The big news this week was West Virginia Senator Joe Manchin finally stopped holding up an energy climate tax bill, the Inflation Reduction Act. If passed, what are some of the critical climate provisions in that bill?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "We are scheduled to talk at the end of July.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The big news this week was that West Virginia Senator Joe Manchin ended his opposition to the energy climate tax bill known as the Inflation Reduction Act.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The listener expects to discuss the Inflation Reduction Act during their upcoming conversation.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The Inflation Reduction Act contains significant climate-related policy measures worth discussing.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The passage of the Inflation Reduction Act is plausible enough to warrant discussion of its potential impact.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 80548,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/0/80548.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 3,
    "turn_text": "Well, if we can get this across the finish line and we're not there yet, but it would be far and away the biggest thing ever done on climate. There are an enormous suite of tax incentives and other programs that could reduce the US's pollution output in terms of carbon dioxide by as much as 40% between now and 2030. We've just never moved the needle that much. And so there's tax credits for wind and solar. There's a whole suite of policies designed at bringing manufacturing for all those clean technologies, batteries, wind, solar, storage, back to the US. There are rebates to help individual families with their cost of their energy burden and really getting them the best, most efficient technologies now at the same price as old-fashioned technology. And so then they can save on their bills month after month after month. So there's an enormous amount in this bill. Obviously it's the result of years worth of negotiating and compromise, but a giant step forward for the climate.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
