    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 80548,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/0/80548.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 33,
    "turn_text": "Well, first, I think it's worth saying that this is the most progress we've made on gun safety in three decades. And having been there for so many battles that went nowhere, I am really encouraged by this because I saw and participated with people in the same room who wanted to do something that would actually save lives. And I saw members who in the past really didn't have a lot of interest in engaging in these conversations, takes in very brave votes and stand up to their own constituents in ways that were very uncomfortable. So I want to give credit that this is a big deal. One, it's the biggest investment in mental health in modern history. Two, on the law enforcement side, it does a lot of things that are really important. that will save lives. Straw purchases is a place where I really engaged in the negotiations. I worked with Senator Susan Collins to figure out how we fix the problem where someone who is prohibited from owning a gun, because they've committed a felony or a violent crime, they get someone else to buy that gun for them. and we can't effectively prosecute that transfer. That is a giant problem and we've fixed it in this legislation. In addition, we always thought as a country that gun trafficking, that that was a problem and always going to be a problem of guns coming into the United States illegally. Well, as we all know in the last decade, the problem has been guns purchased in the United States, traveling to Mexico and countries further south, and then being used by cartels and criminals to commit crimes. And we were able to really fix that once and for all. The exports are now going to be caught up in the law around gun trafficking as well. One more thing I would mention that is really critical is that historically the whole database for when you go and do a background check and you purchase a firearm, if you're a domestic abuser and you're married to your partner, you're in that database and you're not going to get that gun. But for years, there's been a work of basically a loophole where if you're not married, but you're also convicted of abuse, you were still able to buy a firearm. And we have finally plugged that loophole. And I've got to believe that that will literally save from everything we know about the propensity of domestic abuse leading to leading to fatalities, that will save thousands of",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
